OpenAI is the company that basically brought AI into normal people’s lives. They’re the ones behind ChatGPT, and over the last few years they’ve kept pushing out stronger models. They’ve also gotten serious about coding tools. Their main one right now is called Codex. It can write code, find bugs, look over pull requests, and even handle longer jobs on its own — either in the cloud or right on your computer.AI coding assistants overall have become pretty standard for most developers. GitHub Copilot was the first one a lot of people actually used every day inside their editor. It suggests lines as you type and lets you ask questions about the code. The field’s more competitive now though. Claude Code from Anthropic has taken the lead in a lot of the recent surveys for what people reach for most. Cursor and OpenAI’s own Codex are climbing fast too.The real effect is straightforward. These tools help you write code quicker and skip a lot of the boring repetitive stuff. People finish tasks faster, but that doesn’t always mean more finished work actually ships. A good chunk of the time you save writing ends up getting spent checking and cleaning up what the AI gave you. So the job is shifting. Less pure typing, more reviewing, steering, and deciding what’s actually solid.That’s pretty much where we are right now with OpenAI and these AI coding tools. They’re already changing how software gets made, and they keep getting better every few months.
